 "assumptions": [
  {
   "id": "A1",
   "text": "Accepted by Sam (msg 2027): SeededEntropy (repeatable randomness for the web preview and the fake product's differential test, never for tokens) counts as a test double, so it moves into kkt-entropy behind `testing`, not to an implementation crate."
  },
  {
   "id": "A2",
   "text": "Accepted by Sam (msg 2027): kkt-service's DryRun wraps another System and only prints what it would do; it is the crate's own logic over its contract, not a real implementation, so it stays. It records changes in a list and passes reads through, with no effects of its own."
  },
  {
   "id": "A3",
   "text": "Changed by Sam (msg 2027): a crate or program counts as a user (consumer) of a contract only if its code calls the contract's methods. Naming a trait to wire an implementation in (for example Box<dyn watch::Server>, to pick Remote or AdminRemote), or to implement it in an adapter, is not use. Recounted from the code: the trait table lists, for each contract, the crates that only wire it in. No contract crosses the line of more than two users either way. Clock goes from 14 users to 13 (kkt-server only wires it). Entropy goes from 5 to 3 (kkt-server and the fake product only wire it), and to 5 in the proposal, so it stays a shared contract. kkt-watch's Server goes from 1 user beyond its declarer to 0 (the program only picks one): kkt-watch itself, which calls it in watch::run, is its one user, so it still declares it. kkt-admission keeps Entropy: SharedKey::generate draws new access keys with it (shared_key.rs:38), besides the session IDs that move to kkt-web."
  },
  {
   "id": "A4",
   "text": "Moot (msg 2027). (Moot since msg 2017: with kkt-store merged into kkt-rules there are no copies.) \"The record types Reads and Writes use\" was taken as the 36 public types of kkt-store that the two traits' methods name (32), or that appear inside those types (AgentLaneRecord, ChannelNoteRecord, LaneSourceRecord) or inside StoreResult (StoreError)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A5",
   "text": "Moot (msg 2027). (Moot since msg 2017.) kkt-rules no longer named kkt-store's Db: its Deps and CommandLine took a store meeting its Reads and Writes. Now Deps and CommandLine take kkt-rules' own Db."
  },
  {
   "id": "A7",
   "text": "Decided by Sam (msg 2022): kkt-web-preview counts as a program, like kkt-fake-product: a dev-only package whose library exists for its own example and tests. So its Preview implements the parts of kkt-api the page uses (msg 2031), and its PreviewGate implements kkt-web's Gate over kkt-web's sessions (msg 2021)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A8",
   "text": "Replaced by Sam's ruling (msg 2025): kkt-server declares its own one-method contract for its web gate, WebIdentify (which operator is this principal, in which swarm), and kkt-mcp keeps its own Identify. The program wires the same KeyMode into both, adapting it to each (3 and 1 methods). No shared trait and no exception. (Msg 2022's description was based on a stale checkout: kkt-server's AdmissionGate does ask the identity, kkt-review-373, msg 2024.)"
  },
  {
   "id": "A9",
   "text": "Accepted by Sam (msg 2042): kkt-search sits in Foundations: it is pure text that the data crates build on and knows nothing of them, as kkt-clock and kkt-entropy sit under the crates that use them (msg 2014 left the layer to us)."
  },
  {
   "id": "A10",
   "text": "Accepted by Sam (msg 2042): Excerpt stays in kkt-db as a plain value: it is part of what a caller asks for in a Search, and msg 2014 has kkt-store's code (now kkt-rules') use only Search and Hit. kkt-search's excerpt takes the marks and window as arguments, so kkt-db needs kkt-search only under `testing`, for its in-memory backend, and kkt-rules does not name kkt-search. Recommended by kkt-review-373 (msg 2018); msg 2014 listed Excerpt among what moves, so this is for Sam."
  },
  {
   "id": "A11",
   "text": "Accepted by Sam (msg 2042): Whether a message's author, or a channel's last speaker, is the operator is part of kkt-api's records (Message, ChannelActivity), filled by kkt-rules, which makes the operator an actor and names it. With kkt-web rendering kkt-api's records directly (msg 2031), the flag belongs with the record, not with each surface. kkt-web's live notices, which come from kkt-events, still work it out with kkt-names' is_operator."
  },
  {
   "id": "A13",
   "text": "Changed by Sam (msg 2042): the Admin trait moves to its own crate, kkt-api-admin. The rest stands as charted, pending Sam, and the Operator trait's side is on hold. kkt-api's traits are split by area as listed, shaped from what kkt-rules' Rules offers and what kkt-mcp, kkt-web (through kkt-server's former RulesSource), KeyMode and the fake product call. Operator holds what only the web page's operator does, and Admin what /mcp-admin reads, so the page and the preview need only Api, Operator, Lanes, Server and Channels. subscribe returns kkt-events' subscription, so kkt-api names that type. The plain register and reclaim, which only tests call, are left out."
  },
  {
   "id": "A14",
   "text": "Decided for record_run (msg 2042, option (a)): kkt-rules' start records the run in the Db, then builds and returns the Rules; the programs call it and pass the Rules into kkt-server's Parts. record_run is not in kkt-api, since the run_id goes into the Deps that build the Rules (kkt-review-373, msg 2036). Still pending Sam: the fake product's second swarm, charted as kkt-rules' create_swarm, an assembly function over the Db."
  }
 ],
